FerrionOS change to upstream RedoxFS (MIT license, kept), made against upstream commit 5ba45a6887374ab425326656a276bb3b62eba7dc after the existing recipe patches. Checked by the deterministic simulation in sim/redoxfs (sim/redoxfs/run.sh SEEDS OPS --flush-on-unmount).

ferrion-write-barrier.patch flushes the disk after the data and after the header of every commit. That is what a disk FerrionOS runs from needs, but a host image build through a FUSE mount commits thousands of times and each flush is an fsync of the image file: an install took 14 times as long. `redoxfs --flush-on-unmount` turns the per-commit flushes off for that mount (FileSystem::barriers, true unless the caller clears it) and flushes once after the unmount cleanup; a power loss during such a mount can lose the image, which a build makes again. Both mount loops now flush once after their cleanup. A key-slot change still flushes before it returns, so no old slot is left only in a cache. Formatting flushes after each erased header that was valid, then once after the whole ring, instead of after every slot.

